.TH DD 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
dd \- copy a file, converting and formatting it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dd
.RB [ if=\c
.IR file ]
.RB [ of=\c
.IR file ]
.RB [ bs=\c
.IR n ]
.RB [ ibs=\c
.IR n ]
.RB [ obs=\c
.IR n ]
.RB [ count=\c
.IR n ]
.RB [ skip=\c
.IR n ]
.RB [ seek=\c
.IR n ]
.RB [ conv=\c
.IR list ]
.RB [ iflag=\c
.IR list ]
.RB [ oflag=\c
.IR list ]
.RB [ status=\c
.IR word ]
.br
.B dd \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dd
copies its input to its output in blocks, and can skip, limit, pad, and
byte-swap the data on the way.
It reads standard input unless
.BI if= file
is given, and writes standard output unless
.BI of= file
is given.
Its arguments are operands, described under
.BR OPTIONS .
.PP
When the copy ends, normally or after a write error,
.B dd
writes two lines on standard error:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
2+1 records in
2+1 records out
.EE
.RE
.PP
Each line is a count of complete blocks, a plus sign, and a count of
partial blocks.
The first line counts blocks read, in units of
.B ibs
bytes, and the second counts blocks written, in units of
.B obs
bytes.
A block read or written short, as at the end of the input, is partial.
A write to a closed pipe, or any other error, ends
.B dd
at once without the counts.
.B status=none
also suppresses them.
.SS Copying
.B dd
opens the input and then the output, and positions them as
.B skip
and
.B seek
ask.
It then repeats: read one block of up to
.B ibs
bytes, apply the conversions, and write the data.
It stops at the end of the input, or after
.B count
blocks.
.PP
A read from a pipe or a console can return less than
.B ibs
bytes.
Without
.BR iflag=fullblock ,
.B dd
counts that as a block and, if
.B ibs
and
.B obs
are equal, writes it as it is.
When they differ,
.B dd
collects the data and writes blocks of exactly
.B obs
bytes, then the remainder at the end.
.SS Numbers
A number is decimal digits with an optional suffix that multiplies it.
Signs, white space, and hexadecimal and octal notation are not accepted;
.B 010
is ten.
Suffixes are case-sensitive:
.BR c " (1), " w " (2), " b " (512),"
.BR k " or " K " (1024), " kB " or " KB " (1000),"
.BR M " (1048576), " MB " (1000000),"
.BR G " (1073741824), and " GB " (1000000000)."
.BR kD ", " KD ", " MD ", and " GD
are synonyms for
.BR kB ", " KB ", " MB ", and " GB .
A number that is malformed or out of range ends
.B dd
with a message and status 1.
The range is 1 to 2147483647 for
.BR bs ,
.BR ibs ,
and
.BR obs ,
and 0 to 9223372036854775807 for
.BR count ,
.BR skip ,
and
.BR seek .
.SH OPTIONS
Every argument is an operand of the form
\fIkeyword\fB=\fIvalue\fR.
Keywords and the words in their values are case-sensitive.
An argument without an
.B =
or with an unknown keyword prints the usage message on standard error and
exits with status 1.
The operands apply in order, so a repeated keyword takes its last value,
except that
.BR conv ,
.BR iflag ,
.BR oflag ,
and
.B status
accumulate.
The first three take a comma-separated list, as in
.BR conv=notrunc,sync ;
an unknown or empty item is an error.
The argument
.B \-\-
is ignored wherever it appears.
.TP
.BI if= file
Read
.I file
instead of standard input.
A
.I file
named
.B \-
is an ordinary file, not standard input.
.TP
.BI of= file
Write
.I file
instead of standard output.
The file is created if it does not exist.
It is truncated when opened, unless
.B seek
is nonzero or
.B conv=notrunc
is given.
.TP
.BI bs= n
Read and write
.I n
bytes at a time.
This sets both
.B ibs
and
.BR obs ,
so
.B ibs=512 bs=4k
reads 4096 bytes at a time, and
.B bs=4k ibs=512
reads 512 and writes 4096.
.TP
.BI ibs= n
Read
.I n
bytes at a time.
The default is 512.
.TP
.BI obs= n
Write
.I n
bytes at a time.
The default is 512.
.TP
.BI count= n
Copy at most
.I n
input blocks.
A block read short counts as a block.
With
.BR iflag=count_bytes ,
.I n
is a number of bytes.
Without
.BR count ,
.B dd
copies until the end of the input, and
.B count=0
copies nothing.
.TP
.BI skip= n
Skip
.I n
blocks of input before copying.
A seekable input is repositioned.
Any other input, such as a pipe, is read and the data discarded, stopping
early at the end of the input.
Each read counts as a block, so without
.B iflag=fullblock
a short read from a pipe skips fewer bytes.
With
.BR iflag=skip_bytes ,
.I n
is a number of bytes, and an input that cannot seek is read one byte at a
time.
.TP
.BI seek= n
Skip
.I n
blocks of
.B obs
bytes on the output before writing.
Unless
.B conv=notrunc
is given, an output file is first truncated, or extended with zero
bytes, to exactly that offset, so only the data before it survives.
Seeking past the end of a file on NTFS makes the file sparse.
With
.BR oflag=seek_bytes ,
.I n
is a number of bytes.
The output must be seekable: standard output that is a pipe fails with
.B Invalid seek
and status 1.
.TP
.B conv=notrunc
Do not truncate the output file when opening it or seeking.
Data beyond what is written stays in the file.
.TP
.B conv=noerror
Continue after a read error.
.B dd
reports the error, seeks
.B ibs
bytes forward in the input, and goes on, counting a partial block read.
With
.BR sync ,
that block is written as
.B ibs
zero bytes; without it, nothing is written for it.
If the input cannot seek,
.B dd
stops with an error.
Without
.BR noerror ,
a read error ends
.B dd
at once with status 1.
.TP
.B conv=sync
Pad a short input block with zero bytes to
.B ibs
bytes before writing it.
.TP
.B conv=fsync
Flush the output file to disk before finishing.
With different
.B ibs
and
.BR obs ,
the flush comes before the final short output block is written.
It fails when the output is
.I /dev/null
or a console.
.TP
.B conv=swab
Swap each pair of bytes of the input.
An odd byte at the very end is left as it is.
Every block but the last must have an even size, or
.B dd
stops with
.BI "can't swab " n " byte buffer"
and status 1.
Use an even
.B ibs
with
.B iflag=fullblock
when the input is a pipe.
.TP
.B iflag=skip_bytes
Count
.B skip
in bytes instead of blocks.
.TP
.B iflag=count_bytes
Count
.B count
in bytes instead of blocks.
The last block is shortened to fit.
.TP
.B oflag=seek_bytes
Count
.B seek
in bytes instead of blocks.
.TP
.B iflag=fullblock
Repeat reads until the block is full or the input ends, so that only the
last block can be partial.
.TP
.B oflag=append
Open the output file in append mode, so every write goes to the end of
the file.
Without
.BR conv=notrunc ,
the file is still truncated first, to the
.B seek
offset, which is 0 by default.
It has no effect on standard output.
.TP
.B status=noxfer
Accepted and ignored.
This
.B dd
never writes a transfer rate.
.TP
.B status=none
Do not write the record counts.
Error messages are still written.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when busybox starts, as needed
when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
Each operand begins with a keyword and
.BR = ,
so only file names that begin that way can match.
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
A file mode creation mask in octal.
When it includes the owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
an output file that
.B dd
creates is read-only.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES .
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid operand, number, or list item, a file that could not be
opened, read, written, positioned, truncated, flushed, or closed, a swab
failure, or too little memory.
.TP
.B 141
The output is a pipe that was closed before all data was written.
No record counts are written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write one mebibyte of zero bytes to a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dd if=/dev/zero of=blank.img bs=1M count=1
1+0 records in
1+0 records out
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print 16 random bytes as a hex string:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dd if=/dev/urandom bs=16 count=1 status=none | xxd \-p
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy 100 bytes from offset 1000 of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dd if=in of=out skip=1000 count=100 iflag=skip_bytes,count_bytes
.EE
.RE
.PP
Overwrite the bytes at offset 0x1000 of a file with the contents of
another, leaving the rest of the file alone:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dd if=new of=data seek=4096 oflag=seek_bytes conv=notrunc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Extract the first sector of a compressed disk image.
A pipe can deliver less than a full block, and
.B iflag=fullblock
keeps the one block that
.B count=1
allows from coming up short.
.B gzip
may report a broken pipe, since
.B dd
stops reading after that block:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ gzip \-dc disk.gz | dd bs=512 count=1 iflag=fullblock >sector
.EE
.RE
.PP
Swap the bytes of each 16-bit word in a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dd if=big of=little bs=64k conv=swab
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR dd :
.IP \(bu 2
There is no transfer rate line, and no status report on a signal.
.IP \(bu 2
Numbers take no
.B x
multiplier, as in
.BR 2x512 ,
and the only suffixes are those listed under
.BR Numbers .
.IP \(bu 2
.B bs
sets
.B ibs
and
.B obs
where it appears, so a later
.B ibs
or
.B obs
overrides it.
.IP \(bu 2
Operands and values not described above are rejected, such as
.BR cbs ,
.BR status=progress ,
the
.B direct
flags, and the
.B conv
values
.BR ucase ,
.BR lcase ,
.BR block ,
.BR unblock ,
.BR ascii ,
.BR ebcdic ,
.BR excl ,
.BR nocreat ,
.BR fdatasync ,
and
.BR sparse .
.PP
Standard input and output are binary, so CR LF pairs and Ctrl-Z
characters are copied unchanged.
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
A directory as
.B if
fails with
.BR "Permission denied" ,
and as
.B of
with
.BR "Is a directory" .
.PP
For both
.B if
and
.BR of ,
the names
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/tty ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.IR /dev/stdout ,
and
.I /dev/stderr
work as on Unix, with
.I /dev/tty
the console.
.I /dev/fd/n
gives descriptor
.I n
itself rather than a copy, and
.B dd
closes it once it has moved it to standard input or output, so
.B of=/dev/fd/2
loses the record counts and later error messages.
.I /dev/zero
and
.I /dev/urandom
are sources of zero bytes and of random bytes from the system's
cryptographic generator;
as
.B of
they discard the data, like
.IR /dev/null .
.PP
An
.B of
that names a physical drive, such as
.I \e\e.\ePhysicalDrive1
or
.IR //./PhysicalDrive1 ,
is opened without creating or truncating it.
A drive cannot be truncated, so
.B seek
on a drive needs
.BR conv=notrunc ,
or
.B dd
stops with an error before copying.
.PP
When the output is a console, each block is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later,
and escape sequences, such as those for color, take effect.
A block that contains a NUL byte is written unconverted.
Redirect the output to a file or a pipe when copying binary data.
Data read from a console is converted from the console's input code page
to the ANSI code page, unless the ANSI code page is UTF-8.
.PP
The buffers for
.B ibs
and
.B obs
are allocated before copying starts, and a size that exceeds the
available memory ends
.B dd
with
.B out of memory
and status 1.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR head (1),
.BR od (1),
.BR truncate (1),
.BR xxd (1)
